GroovyMAME nogpu: optional RGB565 pixel format (-mister_rgb_mode rgb565)

Adds the 5th CMD_INIT byte (rgbMode) that the Groovy_MiSTer protocol already defines
(groovy.cpp accepts a 4- or 5-byte CMD_INIT; rgbMode 2 = RGB565, 2 bytes per pixel,
little-endian) and that the MiSTer core already implements. GroovyMAME's own nogpu
client never sent it, so it could only stream RGB888.

Why: RGB565 is 2/3 of the RGB888 bandwidth. On a 100 Mbit receiver (Groovy_TaNGer,
Tang Primer 20K) raw RGB888 does not fit a 320x240@60 beam race (116 Mbit/s on the
wire), RGB565 does (77 Mbit/s). On MiSTer it also lowers bandwidth and latency.

- new option mister_rgb_mode: rgb888 (default, unchanged behaviour) or rgb565
- rgb888 keeps the original 4-byte CMD_INIT, so older servers are unaffected
- rgb565: CMD_INIT is 5 bytes with rgbMode = 2; pixels are packed from MAME's B,G,R,A
  bytes as ((R & F8) << 8) | ((G & FC) << 3) | (B >> 3), sent low byte first
- frame sizes (raw send, lz4, lz4hc) use the bytes per pixel instead of a fixed 3

Base: substring/GroovyMAME 4ce01b9 (libretro-driver). Apply with
  git apply -p1 groovymame-mister-rgb565.patch
Checked: applies cleanly; drawnogpu.cpp and osdobj_common.cpp compile (g++ -std=c++20
-Werror -fsyntax-only). Runs on Groovy_TaNGer (Tang Primer 20K to a 15 kHz CRT), not
tested here on a MiSTer.

